MAUS Privacy Policy

MAUS has no telemetry and no user account. It does not collect personal data and sends nothing to its developers.

Data kept on your PC only: your settings, score history, test results, error logs and the log of changes made by MAUS (so they can be undone), in %ProgramData%\MAUS and %LocalAppData%\MAUS. Audit summaries also keep the names of startup programs and logon tasks, and the names and versions of non-Microsoft drivers, to show what changed between two audits. The health log of the Follow page (health-log.json) keeps disk model names, disk wear and errors, battery capacity, boot durations and cooling test results, dated, on your PC only. The hardware card (model names of your screens, memory, graphics cards and disks, never a serial number) stays in %LocalAppData%\MAUS\hardware-card.json. The past battery capacity, read when you click its button, comes from a report that Windows writes in %LocalAppData%\MAUS\rapports\rapport-batterie.xml: this file holds the name of the PC, MAUS keeps nothing from it and deletes it after reading it (if an antivirus holds it, it stays until your next click replaces it). MAUS never reads the BitLocker recovery key; the identifier of that key, which is not secret, is shown on screen only and is never written to a report, an export or a copy. You can delete these folders at any time.

Network: this program will not transfer any information to other networked systems unless specifically requested by the user or the person installing or operating it. Network access happens only for actions you start. During an audit you run: Windows Update search (Microsoft) and software update list (winget); this includes the checking audit that follows a fix, an undo, or granting administrator rights when that option is on. The optional weekly audit skips both and uses no network. Also: Windows repair (DISM can use Windows Update), software upgrades and the optional PawnIO driver through winget, connection test (pings to your router, Cloudflare 1.1.1.1 and Google 8.8.8.8), Wi-Fi walk (your router only), web searches (search engine of your choice), and links that open in your browser (MAUS help site, GitHub, vendor sites, YouTube). Some of these links carry a hardware model in their address; nothing is sent unless you open the link.

